Starting on an actual pattern matching implementation based on the discussion: https://github.com/DanielXMoore/Civet/discussions/150

- Create parallel `MatchingPattern` rules similar to `BindingPattern`
- Use them inside `switch` statements or as part of a TBD "match operator"
- Match based on structure, regexp, inheritance, relational (`> 3`, `!= null`)
- Attach bindings to other properties
- Generate `if` conditionals with `const` bindings in blocks

```typescript
  switch obj
    [code is 200, result]
      // obj is array-like of length 2
      console.log(code, result) // code matched 200 and was bound, result was also bound
    {code: 200, result}
      // obj has properties code and result
      // code is 200 and is available in this block
      // result is bound in this block
    <? Result and {value}
      // obj is an instance of Result
      // value property of obj is bound in this block
    <? Error
      // obj is an instance of error
```

Lots more possibilities but those are the broad strokes.

